twists.in_wave_decisions:
Every few seconds the player decides:
- which way each gate points for the next group of enemies;
- how to split a mixed group when its members cannot all reach their best lane;
- whether to overload a full zone or send enemies into a weaker one;
- whether to send a dangerous enemy down the long loop, where more towers hit it, or down the short channel to spare a weak zone;
- how to answer enemies that interact with gates (one that jams a gate for a few seconds, one that splits at a junction).
Between waves it shapes placement: which tower family owns which channel, and whether to buy gate upgrades (faster swing, one auto-sort rule) instead of towers.

design_lessons:
Teach through play:
- first tower within about 10s, first enemy defeated by about 20s, first wave cleared by about 60s;
- one new idea per wave for the first 4-5 waves, the TD equivalent of Color Block Jam's intro levels;
- at most two contextual prompts and no walls of text.

Telegraph the twist completely:
- each gate shows its current direction with a big arrow and visible water flow;
- each gate previews its next 2-3 arrivals as icons;
- routing is fully deterministic.
This is also the readability layer when sound is muted.

Treat readability as a system:
- every enemy family has a unique silhouette, colour and behaviour, never colour alone (Apple HIG);
- use a colour-blind-safe trio of hues;
- set budgets for entities and particles;
- late-game power should concentrate into fewer, bigger effects;
- bloom debris sits on a quieter visual layer than live enemies.

Tie placement to routing:
- put some tower pads where two channels run close, so a tower can cover both lanes;
- give channels different lengths, so choosing a route means trading exposure against speed.

Shape sessions for phones:
- waves of 30-60s and a level of 12-15 minutes;
- autosave after every wave, with instant resume;
- the player starts each wave, with an early-call bonus;
- pause anywhere, 1x and 2x speed.

Design for portrait and one thumb:
- controls in the bottom third, with touch targets of at least 44pt;
- a gate dock of buttons in the thumb zone mirrors every gate;
- the placement ghost is offset above the finger, with nudge and confirm-on-release.
The core verb should need no drag precision, unlike Galaxy Defense, which is marketed as portrait but needs two hands.

Layer the feedback ('juice') and ASMR so each event lands at once in sight, sound and touch:
- each material gets its own sound, with 3-5 variants and small pitch changes (Unpacking's foley approach);
- tower hits are tuned to a musical scale over a calm music bed (Mini Motorways);
- haptics are sparing and optional: placement, upgrade, wave cleared, enemy getting through.

Build replay into one map:
- several workable ways to assign tower families to channels;
- star and mastery goals;
- restriction challenges and daily seeded modifiers.
BTD6 veterans say weekly challenges, not the campaign, keep them playing.

Offer three difficulty levels:
- Relaxed, for calm players;
- Standard;
- a pure Flow challenge with no flipping while paused and no purchases, like BTD6's CHIMPS mode.

Prototype metrics to track:
- time to first tower;
- share of players who clear wave 1 without help;
- whether players use a gate unprompted by wave 3;
- whether routing quality measurably changes the outcome;
- whether a busy wave stays readable at 375px wide with sound muted;
- whether players start a second run with a different build.
